# UI rules

Tokens live in `tokens.css`. Use the variables, never raw hex.

## Palette intent

- `--teal` in range · `--low` 3.0–3.9 · `--very-low` under 3.0 · `--amber` 10.0–13.9 ·
  `--very-high` over 13.9
- `--gap` is reserved for **absence of data**. Never use it to encode a value.
- `--indigo` is interactive, insulin, and AI surfaces.

## Typography

- Display `Bricolage Grotesque`, body `Public Sans`, all numerals `IBM Plex Mono`.
- Every glucose numeral uses `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`. Values must not
  reflow as they update.
- One decimal place, always.

## CoverageStrip — required, not optional

```
props: { segments: [{ from, to, covered }], period: [start, end] }
```

A 5px track beneath **every chart and every aggregate metric block**. Covered runs
`--teal`, gaps `--gap`. Caption below in mono 10px: period left, coverage right. When
gaps exist, prefix the summary in `--low` — `1 gap · 68 min · 94%`.

This is the component that makes the app trustworthy. Do not make it collapsible,
optional, or conditional on there being gaps.

## GlucoseChart

- Target band `--band` from 3.9 to 10.0, behind gridlines.
- Trace `--ink`, 1.9–2.2px, round caps and joins.
- Below-range points get a 2.1px `--low` dot. Above-range get a smaller `--amber` dot at
  reduced opacity. Highs are common, lows are what you scan for — visual weight should
  reflect that asymmetry.
- Gaps render as hatched fill with no trace, labelled `gap`.
- Y-axis labels only at 3.9, 10.0, and the ceiling.

## MetricGrid

Two columns, 1px `--line` gutters. Cell is mono uppercase key (10px), tabular value
(23px), delta or qualifier (10px). Delta colour is direction-dependent — the metric
definition owns whether up is good, not the component.

## AI-generated text

- Always in the indigo card treatment, visually distinct from computed values.
- Always timestamped.
- Always one tap from the aggregates it was derived from.
- Never inline with a number as though it were part of the data.

## Copy

- Sentence case. No title case anywhere in the UI.
- Name things by what the user controls: "No data for 20 min", not "Stale threshold".
- Buttons state what happens; the result echoes the verb.
- Errors explain what happened and what to do. They do not apologise and are never vague.
- Empty states are an invitation to act.
